---
import PostLayout from "../../layouts/PostLayout.astro";
import PostSection from "../../components/post/PostSection.astro";
import Reveal from "../../components/post/Reveal.astro";
import CaseFile from "../../components/post/CaseFile.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/post/Checklist.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/post/Sources.astro";

const rituals = [
  {
    "key": "done-paragraph",
    "tag": "Knowledge gap · state the intent",
    "title": "Write down what “done” means before anyone codes.",
    "body": "For the metering fix, it would have said: “Future invoices use the corrected meter. Invoices finance has already closed stay exactly as charged.” The standard holder edits it in ten minutes, and that second sentence would have stopped the backfill."
  },
  {
    "key": "ugly-demo",
    "tag": "Alignment gap · backbrief",
    "title": "Demo the rough version on day two.",
    "body": "On day two of a two-week feature, put the rough version in front of the standard holder, with real data, broken styling and no slides. If they say “that’s grouped by the wrong date” now, it costs a conversation. If they say it at launch, it costs a rewrite. AI tools make the rough version a few hours of work, so there’s no reason to wait."
  },
  {
    "key": "unintended-review",
    "tag": "Effects gap · check what it did",
    "title": "Ask what else the diff changes.",
    "body": "Read the diff for what it changes that the description doesn’t mention. In the demo at the top, the rename was perfect and the deleted unsubscribe check was the bug. Then ask “what should be true for this to fail?” and write that test."
  },
  {
    "key": "absence-alerts",
    "tag": "Effects gap · watch the results",
    "title": "Alert when expected data stops arriving.",
    "body": "Add an alert for data that should exist and doesn’t, like “no orders from this region in 24 hours,” and one for data that shouldn’t exist, like “an invoice dated next year.” The pipeline that ingested nothing for six weeks never threw an error."
  },
  {
    "key": "attention-rca",
    "tag": "Knowledge gap · find who knew",
    "title": "Ask who could have seen it, not who missed it.",
    "body": "Skip “who missed it?” Ask “what were we looking at when it slipped, and who would have seen it?” If the answer is “finance would have, and nobody asked them,” the fix is putting finance in the plan review, not warning the engineer."
  },
  {
    "key": "write-it-down",
    "tag": "Alignment gap · brief in writing",
    "title": "Turn the talk into PRDs, RFCs and ADRs as you grow.",
    "body": "Once the team outgrows one room, the done paragraph becomes a PRD. A big change gets a one-page RFC that a peer reviews before anyone builds it. Each settled decision gets a short ADR, so six months later a new engineer can read why the system works this way instead of rediscovering it."
  },
  {
    "key": "fix-pattern",
    "tag": "Effects gap · adjust the plan",
    "title": "Fix every place the same assumption lives.",
    "body": "When a blind spot bites once, search for the same assumption everywhere else. If one integration broke because it trusted a webhook to always arrive, check every integration that trusts a webhook before the second one breaks."
  }
];
---

<PostLayout
  slug="inattentional-blindness"
  kicker="Inattentional blindness · root-caused"
  headline="Your startup isn’t slow. It’s blind."
  dek="Good engineers miss obvious things, the misses compound into slow roadmaps, and a two-person structure catches what one pair of eyes can’t."
  shareText="Your startup isn't slow. It's blind. — on inattentional blindness in engineering teams"
>

    <PostSection id="demo">
      <p class="lede">
        Before I argue anything, review a pull request with me.
      </p>
      <p>
        A teammate renamed <code>user_items</code> to
        <code>digest_items</code> in the weekly-email module. Your job is the
        usual one. Confirm the rename is complete. There are <b>six
        occurrences</b> of the old name, so check every one. Take it as
        seriously as you would at work, because there&rsquo;s a question at
        the end.
      </p>

      <figure class="diff post-panel" aria-label="A code diff renaming user_items to digest_items">
        <div class="diff-title"><span class="diff-file">notifications/digest.py</span><span class="diff-stat">+6 &minus;7</span></div>
        <pre><code><span class="dl ctx">  def send_weekly_digest(user, stories):</span>
<span class="dl del">-     user_items = []</span>
<span class="dl add">+     digest_items = []</span>
<span class="dl ctx">      for story in stories:</span>
<span class="dl del">-         user_items.append(render_card(story))</span>
<span class="dl add">+         digest_items.append(render_card(story))</span>
<span class="dl ctx">      if user.language != "en":</span>
<span class="dl del">-         user_items = translate(user_items, user.language)</span>
<span class="dl add">+         digest_items = translate(digest_items, user.language)</span>
<span class="dl del">-     if user.unsubscribed:</span>
<span class="dl del">-         return None</span>
<span class="dl ctx">      subject = pick_subject(stories)</span>
<span class="dl del">-     mailer.queue(user.email, subject, user_items)</span>
<span class="dl add">+     mailer.queue(user.email, subject, digest_items)</span>
<span class="dl ctx">      audit_log.record(user.id, subject)</span>
<span class="dl del">-     return DigestReceipt(user_items)</span>
<span class="dl add">+     return DigestReceipt(digest_items)</span></code></pre>
      </figure>

      <Reveal summary="Lock in your answer. Was the rename complete?">
          <p>
            <b>Yes. All six occurrences were renamed, perfectly.</b> And if
            that&rsquo;s what you checked, you almost certainly sailed past the
            two lines in the middle that have nothing to do with the rename:
          </p>
          <pre class="reveal-snippet"><code>-     if user.unsubscribed:
-         return None</code></pre>
          <p>
            This diff also <b>deletes the unsubscribe guard</b>. Everyone who
            opted out starts getting emailed again. The deletion isn&rsquo;t
            hidden. It sits in plain sight, unpaired, in the middle of the
            diff. But your task was to find <code>user_items</code> &rarr;
            <code>digest_items</code> pairs, so your attention ran that
            template, and a deletion outside it didn&rsquo;t register.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you caught it, notice that you were primed. You knew this essay
            was going to test you, and you still felt the pull to stop looking
            once the sixth rename checked out.
          </p>
      </Reveal>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="science" no="01" label="The gorilla, twice" title="Looking is not seeing">
      <p class="gorilla-ink">
        In 1999, Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris asked people to watch a
        video of two teams passing basketballs and count the passes made by the
        team in white. Halfway through, a person in a gorilla suit walks into
        the middle of the frame, thumps their chest, and walks off. It&rsquo;s
        on screen for nine seconds. <b>About half the viewers never see
        it.</b><sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup>
        The follow-up is the one that should worry engineers. In 2013, Trafton
        Drew and colleagues embedded a gorilla into CT scans, about 48 times the
        size of a typical lung nodule, and asked 24 expert
        radiologists to hunt for nodules. <b>Twenty of the 24 never saw
        it.</b> Eye-tracking showed that most of the radiologists who missed it
        looked <em>directly at it</em>.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup>
      </p>

      <figure class="chart post-panel" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: about 50% of untrained viewers miss the gorilla in the basketball video; 83% of expert radiologists missed it in CT scans.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Who misses the gorilla?</figcaption>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 190" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <g font-family="var(--font-ui)" font-size="13">
            <!-- row 1 -->
            <text x="0" y="34" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">Untrained viewers, basketball video</text>
            <rect x="0" y="44" width="560" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--color-line)"></rect>
            <rect x="0" y="44" width="280" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--color-accent)"></rect>
            <text x="292" y="56" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">~50% miss it</text>
            <!-- row 2 -->
            <text x="0" y="104" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">Expert radiologists, CT lung scans</text>
            <rect x="0" y="114" width="560" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--color-line)"></rect>
            <rect x="0" y="114" width="465" height="14" rx="4" fill="var(--color-accent)"></rect>
            <text x="477" y="126" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">83%</text>
            <!-- annotation -->
            <text x="0" y="172" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="12.5">Most radiologists who missed it looked directly at it (eye-tracking).</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>Two details make this finding hard to shrug off:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <b>Expertise doesn&rsquo;t protect you.</b> These were trained
          specialists doing the inspection they do every day. Their training
          didn&rsquo;t widen what they saw. It tightened the template they
          searched with, so anything off the template had less chance to
          register.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Misses aren&rsquo;t laziness.</b> The eye-tracking data rules out
          the comforting story that people miss things because they
          didn&rsquo;t look. You can point your eyes at the bug and still not
          see the bug.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="gorilla-ink gorilla-ink--inverse">
      <p>
        <b>Hard work doesn&rsquo;t protect you either.</b> Attention is a
        finite budget, not a virtue. The harder the primary task, the less
        capacity is left for anything else, even for things that are large,
        unusual and directly in view.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup> Under
        high perceptual load, an unexpected object doesn&rsquo;t register
        dimly. It doesn&rsquo;t register at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 1980, NASA put eight airline pilots in a Boeing&nbsp;727 simulator
        and had them land in low visibility with a head-up display, which
        projects flight data onto the windscreen. On one approach, a plane
        pulled onto the runway in front of them. <b>Two of the eight never saw
        it</b> and kept landing.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup> They
        weren&rsquo;t flying badly. With the display, they held the glide path
        better than without it. Their attention was locked onto the symbols,
        and the symbols said everything was fine.
      </p>
      <p>
        Daniel Kahneman describes the gorilla study in the first chapter of
        <i>Thinking, Fast and Slow</i>, and he draws a line from it that
        belongs on every code-review tool: <b>&ldquo;we can be blind to the
        obvious, and we are also blind to our
        blindness.&rdquo;</b><sup><a href="#src-7">7</a></sup> A reviewer
        skimming a diff at 5&nbsp;p.m. isn&rsquo;t verifying. They&rsquo;re
        matching the diff against the shapes they expect. Kahneman calls the
        trap <em>what you see is all there is</em>. The fast, automatic part
        of the mind builds a coherent story from what&rsquo;s in front of it,
        and it never flags what&rsquo;s missing.
      </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="load-demo post-panel" aria-label="Interactive demo: increasing cognitive load narrows what you can see in a code file.">
        <figcaption class="chart-title">Attention is a budget. Feel it.</figcaption>
        <div class="load-controls">
          <label for="loadSlider">Deadline pressure</label>
          <input id="loadSlider" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="55" />
          <span class="load-readout" id="loadReadout">55%</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="load-code" id="loadCode" style="--load:0.55"><code><span class="cl" style="--d:4"># worker liveness check &mdash; restarts stuck workers</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:3">RECENT = timedelta(minutes=10)</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:2"></span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:1">def health_check(worker):</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:0">    last = worker.last_completed_activity()</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:0">    if now() - last &gt; RECENT:</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:0">        worker.kill()  # assume stuck, force restart</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:1">    </span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:2"># NOTE: nightly reconciliation activities legitimately</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:3"># run 2&ndash;6 hours with no intermediate completions.</span>
<span class="cl" style="--d:4"># They will look "stuck" to any 10-minute heuristic.</span></code></pre>
        <p class="load-caption">
          The lines you&rsquo;re editing stay sharp. The comment that
          invalidates your whole approach sits in the periphery, and load
          deletes the periphery first. Load doesn&rsquo;t make you sloppier.
          It makes you <em>narrower</em>.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Now describe a startup engineer&rsquo;s day in these terms: a
        high-load primary task (ship the feature), time pressure, constant
        context switching, and a strong prior about what the code is supposed
        to do. A sprint is a selective-attention test that runs eight hours a
        day. The gorilla is whatever your task template doesn&rsquo;t match.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="cases" no="02" label="The case files" title="Root-causing a decade of misses">
      <p class="lede">
        I&rsquo;ve seen the same failure again and again over the past decade,
        at Orai, at CopyCat and on teams I&rsquo;ve compared notes with, long
        before AI started writing the code. The files below are composites.
        Every mechanism is real, but I changed the details so none of them
        trace back to one company.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of these came from incompetence. In every one, an attention
        system ran exactly as designed and pointed at the wrong thing. Before
        you open each verdict, ask yourself <em>who was positioned to see this
        one coming?</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        Every verdict names one of two people. A <b>technical peer</b> is a
        second engineer who reads your diff and asks what else it changes. A
        <b>standard holder</b> owns what &ldquo;done&rdquo; and
        &ldquo;correct&rdquo; mean for the work, and they&rsquo;re usually not
        an engineer. A PM, an ops lead or a finance owner judges the result,
        never the code.
      </p>

      <div class="case-grid">
        <CaseFile
          no="01"
          name="A settings update quietly wiped a customer’s discounts"
          staring="the intended change"
          invisible="the unintended one, in the same diff"
          seenBy="the customer"
          catcher="A technical peer."
        >
          <p>
            A customer asked for one small change. Their invoices should show
            prices in euros as well as dollars. An engineer updated the
            integration that writes the customer&rsquo;s billing settings,
            checked that the euro prices appeared, and opened a pull request.
            The reviewer checked the same thing. Nobody noticed that the
            update rewrote the whole settings object instead of one field, so
            it also wiped the customer&rsquo;s volume discounts. For three
            weeks every invoice went out at list price, until the
            customer&rsquo;s biggest account disputed a bill.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">The author reviewed their own diff and saw what they
meant, not what it did. A second engineer who asks &ldquo;what else does this
change?&rdquo; instead of &ldquo;does it do the thing?&rdquo; runs a different template. This
is the demo you just did, in production.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="02"
          name="A pipeline lost weeks of records without one error"
          staring="errors and red dashboards"
          invisible="the records that never arrived"
          seenBy="a sales lead staring at a zero"
          catcher="A standard holder."
        >
          <p>
            A refactor renamed the files a vendor dropped into storage, from
            <code>orders_2026-03-01.csv</code> to
            <code>orders-2026-03-01.csv</code>. The ingestion job still looked
            for underscores. It found no files, and it reported success,
            because zero files isn&rsquo;t an error. For six weeks one
            region&rsquo;s orders never arrived. Every dashboard stayed green,
            since nothing had failed. The monthly revenue report showed that
            region at exactly zero, and a sales lead asked whether the whole
            region had churned.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">An alert only fires on the silence an engineer
thought to predict. But anyone who owned the standard &ldquo;this region sends
roughly this many orders every week&rdquo; would have flagged the first empty week.
Monitoring watches for what we expect to break, and standards watch for what
must stay true.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="03"
          name="A release broke the app for every logged-out user"
          staring="the path we demo"
          invisible="the path we never personally walk"
          seenBy="a prospect clicking a sales email"
          catcher="A technical peer, barely."
        >
          <p>
            A release moved the cookie banner into a component that read the
            user&rsquo;s profile. Logged-in users had a profile, so the page
            rendered. Logged-out visitors didn&rsquo;t, so the page crashed
            before it drew anything. Every engineer, every QA pass and every
            Friday demo ran logged in, because that&rsquo;s how everyone
            inside the company uses the product. The first report came from a
            prospect who clicked through to the pricing page from a sales
            email.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">A reviewer or QA partner who checks the
unglamorous states, like logged out, empty and erroring, covers exactly the
paths an excited author skips. Demo pressure aims everyone&rsquo;s attention at the
same pixels.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="04"
          name="A trailing space crashed the rules engine"
          staring="the logic around the data"
          invisible="a literally invisible character"
          seenBy="whoever finally diffed the bytes"
          catcher="A technical peer."
        >
          <p>
            A rules engine matched each customer&rsquo;s plan name against a
            lookup table to decide their support tier. One afternoon it
            started crashing on a single account. Engineers printed the two
            values side by side, <code>"PREMIUM SUPPORT&nbsp;"</code> and
            <code>"PREMIUM SUPPORT"</code>, and read them as equal for hours.
            Someone had pasted a trailing space into the table. The hunt ended
            when an engineer compared the lengths and got 16 and 15.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">Honestly, this one is the radiology study again. You
can look straight at a defect and not see it. Looking harder doesn&rsquo;t fix that.
Fresh eyes run a different template, and a different template sees a
different gorilla.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="05"
          name="A correct fix put the books out of balance"
          staring="making the math right"
          invisible="what “right” means downstream"
          seenBy="the finance team, at close"
          catcher="A standard holder."
        >
          <p>
            A usage meter double-counted API calls that retried after a
            timeout. Engineering fixed the meter and then did the careful
            thing. They recalculated six months of historical usage so every
            number in the system would be correct. But customers had already
            paid invoices built on the old numbers. The recalculation quietly
            rewrote the usage behind invoices that finance had already closed,
            so the books no longer matched the money in the bank. Engineering
            was right that the old numbers were wrong. Finance was right that
            you can&rsquo;t change what a customer was charged by editing
            history. Nobody had asked finance before the backfill ran.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">Code review can&rsquo;t catch correct code that&rsquo;s aimed at
the wrong definition of correct. The person who owns the standard, &ldquo;the books
match the charges, always,&rdquo; would have caught this in the one-sentence plan,
before anyone wrote a line.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="06"
          name="Nobody knew the customer ran a second tool"
          staring="our system’s behavior"
          invisible="the customer’s world around it"
          seenBy="the customer, once someone asked"
          catcher="A standard holder."
        >
          <p>
            A customer reported duplicate entries in their accounting system,
            and the team spent two days hunting a bug in its own sync. The
            sync was fine. A year earlier, the customer&rsquo;s ops team had
            connected a second tool that pushed the same records, and both
            tools had been writing side by side ever since. No engineer had
            asked what else touched that account, because that isn&rsquo;t an
            engineering question. Anyone in customer success could have
            answered it on one call.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">Domain context is a form of attention. A
non-technical partner in ops, sales or customer success knows how the customer
actually works, and engineering&rsquo;s task template will never include that.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="07"
          name="Two vendor switches, one caught in hours, one by angry users"
          staring="the migration going green"
          invisible="the fields the old vendor supplied for free"
          seenBy="a teammate within hours the first time, app-store reviews the second"
          catcher="A technical peer."
        >
          <p>
                At Orai we switched speech-transcription vendors. The first
                time, a teammate tested the output that same day and noticed
                that a quality metric had flatlined at zero, because the new
                vendor didn&rsquo;t return a confidence field the old one did.
                We rolled back within hours. Two months later we switched again,
                and this time nobody listed what else the old vendor had been
                quietly providing. It turned out to be punctuation, which fed
                our pause and sentence-length detection. Users started hearing
                that their sentences were &ldquo;too long&rdquo; no matter how
                they spoke. We found out from a cluster of angry app-store
                reviews.
              </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">This pair of incidents is the cleanest A/B test I
have. It was the same team, the same integration and the same class of bug.
When a second person watched the output, we caught the miss in hours. When
nobody did, customers caught it. Skill didn&rsquo;t change between the two
switches. Coverage did.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>

        <CaseFile
          no="08"
          name="Previews rendered wrong for a year behind a good demo"
          staring="the demo files we always tested with"
          invisible="everything those files didn’t exercise"
          seenBy="the first person who hunted for gaps instead of confirmation"
          catcher="A standard holder."
        >
          <p>
            At CopyCat we turned Figma designs into working code, and for a
            year the demo looked right. Then someone ran a fidelity audit that
            hunted for gaps instead of confirming the demo. The first gap was
            a missing <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code>, which had dropped
            every preview into the browser&rsquo;s quirks mode. The second was
            text that wrapped a few pixels off on real designs. The third was
            an old positioning &ldquo;fix&rdquo; that solved the one bad case
            anyone had seen and quietly mis-positioned elements on designs
            nobody had tested. The engineer who wrote it explained it
            honestly. They hadn&rsquo;t known the CSS precedence rule, so they
            fixed the failure in front of them, &ldquo;nothing specific to a
            failure condition.&rdquo; All three bugs had been there since the
            first implementation. Our test designs never exercised them, so
            the demo never showed them.
          </p>
          <Fragment slot="verdict">Engineering kept confirming that the code ran. It
took someone whose standard was &ldquo;pixel-true to the design&rdquo; to see what had
been wrong the whole time. That&rsquo;s a designer&rsquo;s definition of correct, and no
compiler checks it.</Fragment>
        </CaseFile>
      </div>

      <p>
        I could keep going. A backfill burned a whole third-party API quota in
        one run. A CopyCat build server filled its disk and died on the
        morning of an investor call. An Orai feature sat dead for months until
        a customer emailed us. Once you have the lens, you stop seeing bad
        luck and start seeing one mechanism.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seniority doesn&rsquo;t protect you either. In one of these files, a
        founder waved off a teammate&rsquo;s warning about a crash-causing
        null, because they were heads-down on a migration. The crash came the
        next morning. Senior people carry more load, and more load makes you
        blinder.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="channels" no="03" label="The taxonomy" title="Two channels of blindness">
      <p>
        Sort every case file above, and every escaped defect you&rsquo;ve
        root-caused, and each one rides one of two channels.
      </p>

      <figure class="diagram post-panel" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a developer at full attentional load produces misses through two channels — technical (code diverges from intent) and alignment (intent diverges from reality) — both shipping and being found late.">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 680 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M 0 1 L 9 5 L 0 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-muted)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <!-- developer node -->
          <rect x="10" y="110" width="180" height="80" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="100" y="143" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="500">One developer</text>
          <text x="100" y="163" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11.5">at full attentional load</text>
          <!-- channel 1 -->
          <line x1="190" y1="130" x2="268" y2="72" stroke="var(--color-muted)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <rect x="272" y="30" width="230" height="84" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="387" y="58" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-accent)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5">CHANNEL 1 · TECHNICAL</text>
          <text x="387" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="13">code &ne; intent</text>
          <text x="387" y="99" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">you can&rsquo;t proofread your own model</text>
          <!-- channel 2 -->
          <line x1="190" y1="170" x2="268" y2="228" stroke="var(--color-muted)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <rect x="272" y="186" width="230" height="84" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-teal)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="387" y="214" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-teal)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5">CHANNEL 2 · ALIGNMENT</text>
          <text x="387" y="236" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="13">intent &ne; reality</text>
          <text x="387" y="255" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">correctly built, correctly wrong</text>
          <!-- converge -->
          <line x1="502" y1="72" x2="580" y2="132" stroke="var(--color-muted)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <line x1="502" y1="228" x2="580" y2="168" stroke="var(--color-muted)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <rect x="584" y="118" width="88" height="64" rx="10" fill="var(--color-accent)" opacity="0.14"/>
          <rect x="584" y="118" width="88" height="64" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="628" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12" font-weight="500">shipped,</text>
          <text x="628" y="163" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12" font-weight="500">found late</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <b>Channel 1: the code drifts from your intent.</b> You can&rsquo;t
        proofread your own change, because you read what you meant instead of
        what you wrote. Copywriters solved this with a copy chief, and a code
        reviewer is yours. The deleted unsubscribe guard in the demo above
        rode this channel. So did a settings update that was meant to add euro
        prices and also wiped a customer&rsquo;s discounts.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Channel 2: your intent drifts from reality.</b> You built your
        reading of the request on assumptions about the need, the data, the
        vendors and the customer&rsquo;s setup. Each assumption drifts a
        little with every decision you make alone. Code review can&rsquo;t
        catch this, because the code is correct. It&rsquo;s just correctly
        wrong. A careful billing fix that rewrote invoices finance had already
        closed rode this channel. So did a &ldquo;sync bug&rdquo; that turned
        out to be the customer&rsquo;s own second tool writing the same
        records.
      </p>
      <p>
        You could split these further, into data drift, vendor drift, untested
        paths and a dozen more. Every split still comes down to one of two
        questions. Does the code do what I meant? Is what I meant still true
        out there? A second engineer can answer the first by reading the diff.
        The second needs someone who lives outside the code.
      </p>
      <p>
        One person can&rsquo;t watch both channels, because watching spends
        the same attention that writing the code does. The better you are at
        the work, the less is left over.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="slowdown" no="04" label="The bill" title="How blindness becomes slowness">
      <p>
        Postmortems file these incidents under <em>quality</em>. The bigger
        cost is <em>speed</em>.
      </p>

      <figure class="diagram post-panel" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of the slowdown loop: a miss ships, is found late, triggers interrupt-driven rework, which raises work-in-progress and context switching, which raises attentional load, which produces more misses.">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 680 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arr2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M 0 1 L 9 5 L 0 9" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <!-- 5 nodes around an ellipse, arrows clockwise -->
          <g font-size="12">
            <rect x="255" y="16" width="170" height="52" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="340" y="38" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">a miss ships</text>
            <text x="340" y="56" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">(either channel)</text>

            <rect x="490" y="105" width="180" height="52" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="580" y="127" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">found late</text>
            <text x="580" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">by a customer, not a test</text>

            <rect x="440" y="255" width="230" height="66" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="555" y="279" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">interrupt-driven rework</text>
            <text x="555" y="297" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">incident channel &middot; damage audit</text>
            <text x="555" y="312" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">customer trust repair</text>

            <rect x="30" y="255" width="220" height="66" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="140" y="283" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">WIP &amp; context-switching rise</text>
            <text x="140" y="301" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">the roadmap waits</text>

            <rect x="15" y="105" width="185" height="52" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="107" y="127" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-weight="500">attentional load rises</text>
            <text x="107" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-accent)" font-size="11">blindness increases</text>
          </g>
          <path d="M 428 60 Q 520 70 566 100" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
          <path d="M 585 160 Q 600 210 575 250" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
          <path d="M 436 290 Q 345 305 256 289" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
          <path d="M 120 250 Q 95 210 105 162" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
          <path d="M 130 100 Q 175 55 250 42" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#arr2)"/>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        At Orai we once switched speech-to-text vendors, and the new one
        didn&rsquo;t send punctuation. Our app used punctuation to measure
        sentence length, so it started telling every user their sentences were
        too long. The fix was one missing field and a few hours of work. The
        cost was the weeks around it. We answered angry app-store reviews,
        cleared the support queue, and won back users we&rsquo;d told to
        shorten sentences that were fine. All of that work arrived as
        interruptions, so the roadmap stalled while the team was busier than
        ever. Busier people carry more load, and more load is how the next
        miss ships. That&rsquo;s the loop in the diagram.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        <b>Nobody tickets this work.</b> No one files a ticket for calming an
        angry reviewer or winning back a user, so it never shows up in a
        sprint, a velocity chart or a postmortem. At a startup it lands on
        whoever is closest. With no support team to absorb it, that&rsquo;s
        usually an engineer or a founder, and the roadmap pays for it without
        anyone seeing the bill.
      </p>
      <p>
        The case files follow the same pattern. Three out of four took <b>a
        day or two to fix</b> once someone looked. What cost months was the
        stretch before anyone did.
      </p>
      <p>
        Boehm and Basili put avoidable rework at <b>40&ndash;50% of the
        effort</b> on a typical software project.<sup><a
        href="#src-6">6</a></sup> Raytheon measured its own. Rework was eating
        about 40% of total project cost, so the company built an improvement
        push around inspections, where a second reader checks each design and
        change. Rework fell to about 20%.<sup><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup> On
        a ten-person team, that&rsquo;s the difference between four people
        redoing work and two.
      </p>
      <p>
        Early-stage teams pay the most, because the habits that make them fast
        are the same ones that raise the load. Each system has one owner, so
        nobody looks twice. Everyone switches context all day. The Friday demo
        points every pair of eyes at the same screen. And the company is too
        young to remember the last time this happened.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="triad" no="05" label="The fix" title="One developer, two watchers">
      <p class="lede">
        <b>A developer does their best work inside a triangle.</b> A technical
        peer checks that the code does what the developer meant. A standard
        holder checks that what they meant is still what&rsquo;s needed. The
        developer builds.
      </p>

      <figure class="diagram post-panel" role="img" aria-label="Triangle diagram: a developer supported by a technical peer watching channel 1 (technical misses) and a standard holder watching channel 2 (alignment misses).">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 680 330" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)">
          <!-- triangle edges -->
          <line x1="340" y1="66" x2="150" y2="240" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="340" y1="66" x2="530" y2="240" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="150" y1="240" x2="530" y2="240" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 5"/>
          <!-- developer -->
          <rect x="255" y="18" width="170" height="56" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-text-soft)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="340" y="42" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="500">the developer</text>
          <text x="340" y="61" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">budget spent on building</text>
          <!-- peer -->
          <rect x="40" y="240" width="230" height="72" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="155" y="264" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-accent)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5">TECHNICAL PEER</text>
          <text x="155" y="284" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">&ldquo;what does this change</text>
          <text x="155" y="300" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">that you didn&rsquo;t intend?&rdquo;</text>
          <!-- standard holder -->
          <rect x="410" y="240" width="240" height="72" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-teal)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="530" y="264" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-teal)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5">STANDARD HOLDER</text>
          <text x="530" y="284" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">&ldquo;is this the thing</text>
          <text x="530" y="300" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">we actually needed?&rdquo;</text>
          <!-- channel tags beside the edges -->
          <rect x="52" y="152" width="158" height="40" rx="10" fill="var(--color-accent)" opacity="0.12"/>
          <text x="131" y="169" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-accent)" font-size="10.5">covers channel 1</text>
          <text x="131" y="184" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="10.5">review &middot; pairing</text>
          <rect x="470" y="152" width="170" height="40" rx="10" fill="var(--color-teal)" opacity="0.14"/>
          <text x="555" y="169" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-teal)" font-size="10.5">covers channel 2</text>
          <text x="555" y="184" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="10.5">demos &middot; definition of done</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <b>The technical peer</b> doesn&rsquo;t ask whether the code is good.
        They ask <em>what does this change do that you didn&rsquo;t
        intend?</em> They bring attention you&rsquo;ve already spent. In the
        demo at the top, they&rsquo;re the one who spots the deleted
        unsubscribe check while you count renames. Inspection research has
        found the same thing for fifty years. A second reader catches defects
        the author can&rsquo;t, because the author reads their own
        intent.<sup><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup> Reviewers have a budget too.
        In Cisco&rsquo;s study of 2,500 reviews, reviewers who went faster
        than about 400 lines an hour found far fewer defects.<sup><a
        href="#src-5">5</a></sup> At one company I&rsquo;ve watched, an
        engineer publicly called the team&rsquo;s best reviewer &ldquo;the
        single largest multiplier to my personal productivity.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        Worried a second reviewer slows you down? In the radiology study,
        every radiologist who spotted the gorilla also found the nodule on
        that same slice.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup> Noticing the odd
        thing didn&rsquo;t cost them the main job. It only costs you when one
        person under load has to do both. A reviewer of this draft asked how
        much slower a second pair of eyes makes things. The study didn&rsquo;t
        time it, but the review data above says the slower pace is what makes
        reviews work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The standard holder</b> is usually a PM, an ops lead, a domain
        expert or a founder. They never read the diff, and that&rsquo;s why
        they help. They look at the invoice instead of the code that makes it,
        so a wrong total jumps out at them that an engineer would scroll past.
        They ask <em>is this the thing we needed?</em>, and they ask it early
        and often. They also decide whose definition of correct wins when two
        teams disagree. When engineering recalculated invoices that finance
        had already closed, one question to finance would have stopped it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>This is also the engineering case for diverse teams.</b> People who
        trained the same way share a template, so they miss the same things.
        The twenty radiologists didn&rsquo;t miss twenty different things.
        They all missed the <em>same</em> gorilla. Facial analysis gave the
        most public example. In 2018, commercial systems misclassified
        darker-skinned women up to <b>34.7%</b> of the time and
        lighter-skinned men less than 1% of the time. Their benchmarks were
        roughly four-fifths lighter-skinned faces, and the teams that built
        and tested them didn&rsquo;t notice. Joy Buolamwini, an MIT grad
        student the systems kept failing on, did.<sup><a
        href="#src-8">8</a></sup> A team that all looks, trains and thinks
        alike is one attention system with extra keyboards.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this means &ldquo;add process.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s two working
        relationships. It also matches one of the oldest findings in software
        engineering. A problem found after delivery can cost 100 times more to
        fix than one caught in requirements or design.<sup><a
        href="#src-6">6</a></sup> The triangle moves the catch earlier. Talent
        doesn&rsquo;t fix attention. <em>Coverage</em> does.
      </p>
      <p>
        As the company grows, the triangle&rsquo;s conversations should start
        leaving documents behind. The standard holder&rsquo;s idea of done
        becomes a PRD. The peer&rsquo;s review moves before the code, into an
        RFC. Settled decisions go into ADRs, so the next engineer knows why
        the system looks the way it does. That&rsquo;s process, but it grows
        out of the triangle. It doesn&rsquo;t replace it.
      </p>

      <h3>What it looks like when it works</h3>
      <p>
        The triangle doesn&rsquo;t need a reorg, because it&rsquo;s a
        rotation, not a hire. People swap corners by problem, not by title. At
        Orai I was the standard holder as COO on some problems and the
        technical peer on others, and our CEO or CTO took whichever corner I
        wasn&rsquo;t in. Here are nine casts that actually ran.
      </p>

