              <text x="10" y="142" text-anchor="start" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">CTO</text>
              <text x="210" y="142" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">designer</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="mini-triad"><span class="mt-co">CopyCat</span>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 220 158" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)" font-size="11.5">
              <line x1="110" y1="36" x2="34" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <line x1="110" y1="36" x2="186" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <line x1="34" y1="122" x2="186" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
              <circle cx="110" cy="36" r="5" fill="var(--color-text-soft)"/>
              <circle cx="34" cy="122" r="5" fill="var(--color-accent)"/>
              <circle cx="186" cy="122" r="5" fill="var(--color-teal)"/>
              <text x="110" y="22" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)">ML engineer</text>
              <text x="10" y="142" text-anchor="start" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">CTO</text>
              <text x="210" y="142" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">marketer</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="mini-triad"><span class="mt-co">CopyCat</span>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 220 158" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)" font-size="11.5">
              <line x1="110" y1="36" x2="34" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <line x1="110" y1="36" x2="186" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <line x1="34" y1="122" x2="186" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
              <circle cx="110" cy="36" r="5" fill="var(--color-text-soft)"/>
              <circle cx="34" cy="122" r="5" fill="var(--color-accent)"/>
              <circle cx="186" cy="122" r="5" fill="var(--color-teal)"/>
              <text x="110" y="22" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)">me &mdash; CAIO</text>
              <text x="10" y="142" text-anchor="start" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">developer</text>
              <text x="210" y="142" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">PM</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="triad-legend">
          <span class="dot dot-b"></span>builder&ensp;
          <span class="dot dot-p"></span>fresh technical eye&ensp;
          <span class="dot dot-s"></span>standard holder
        </p>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Orai spent a year and a half shipping constantly with flat metrics.
        Then, during Techstars, a designer, a developer and a founder started
        meeting every week about one screen, the first lesson a new user sees.
        In about three months, completion of that lesson went from <b>roughly
        10% to 95%</b>. Our archives back the starting number, not the peak,
        so 95% is my best reconstruction. None of the changes involved speech
        technology. We added a banner telling people to hit record, cut text,
        swapped a static walkthrough for an animated one, and put &ldquo;redo
        this lesson&rdquo; and &ldquo;next lesson&rdquo; where a thumb lands.
        The gamified loop we added was the developer&rsquo;s idea, not the
        designer&rsquo;s. The team and its hours didn&rsquo;t change. The loop
        did.
      </p>
      <p>
        CopyCat followed the same shape. For a year our production model sat
        stale while everyone worked hard on everything else. A manual audit in
        late 2022 put component-detection accuracy between <b>29% and 75%</b>,
        depending on element type. Our performance marketer ran the structured
        tests, because fresh eyes don&rsquo;t need the right job title, and ML
        engineers fixed what he found. Progress stalled at 75%, and someone
        reasonably asked, <em>&ldquo;it&rsquo;s good enough, no?&rdquo;</em>
        It wasn&rsquo;t. Below about 95%, every missed component became broken
        code in everything generated on top of it. I held the standard, showed
        the team exactly that, and kept the bar where it was. The
        developer&rsquo;s skill closed the gap, from <b>75% to roughly
        95%</b>. Users called the result &ldquo;a different league&rdquo; and
        said false positives were &ldquo;near absent.&rdquo; It took about
        three months with the loop, after a year without one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here&rsquo;s a smaller one, from a company I&rsquo;ll keep anonymous.
        A new engineer spent their second day sitting with the ops team,
        listening to them complain about one screen. They fixed it on days
        three and four. On day five, the ops lead found the fix live in the
        middle of a demo. That team later called a bigger rebuild of the
        console a game-changer, and their daily use of it shaped what got
        built next. When the team worked the same way with customers, one of
        them wrote in unprompted: &ldquo;oh wow &mdash; that is easy.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        In both companies, the same people worked the same hours through the
        flat stretch and the fast one. Effort never changed. Structure did.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="automation" no="06" label="The objection" title="“Shouldn’t a machine be watching this?”">
      <p class="lede">
        A peer asked me this question after I sent them a draft of this
        essay.
      </p>
      <p>
        He argued that the problem is bigger than any team&rsquo;s attention,
        and that mature industries stopped relying on attention long ago. Your
        car diagnoses itself over a bus protocol. Manufacturing didn&rsquo;t
        fix human error by hiring more careful people. It built jidoka, andon
        cords and pointing-and-calling. <em>Today I pay attention, tomorrow I
        don&rsquo;t.</em> In his words, the structure I&rsquo;m proposing is
        &ldquo;a sliver along a mountain of automations that is
        required.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        He&rsquo;s right, case by case. An acceptance test would have caught
        the settings update that wiped a customer&rsquo;s discounts. An alert
        on missing data would have caught the pipeline that ingested nothing
        for six weeks. A linter would have flagged the trailing space in the
        plan name. The newer tools go further. Mutation testing shows which
        tests would still pass if the code broke. Formal methods like TLA+ and
        Lean prove a property holds for every input, not just the ones you
        tried. AI reviewers read every diff and don&rsquo;t get tired at
        5&nbsp;p.m. Used well, these tools cut the thrash a lot.
      </p>
      <p>
        But every one of them is <b>crystallized attention</b>. Someone chose
        the property to prove, the mutants worth killing, the linter&rsquo;s
        rules and what the AI reviewer should look for. The tools sharpen our
        precision. They don&rsquo;t decide where to aim it, or what
        <em>correct</em> means this quarter. Before product&ndash;market fit
        that definition keeps moving, and a test written against last
        month&rsquo;s definition will happily pass this month&rsquo;s wrong
        code.
      </p>
      <p>
        That&rsquo;s the triangle&rsquo;s job. After each miss, ask what you
        were staring at and who was positioned to see it. Every answer that
        ends in &ldquo;a machine could watch this&rdquo; becomes this
        sprint&rsquo;s test, alert or proof. <b>The triangle isn&rsquo;t the
        alternative to the automation mountain. It&rsquo;s the quarry.</b>
        Pointing-and-calling was a human ritual before it was a system, and
        the ritual is how anyone learned what to automate.
      </p>
      <p>
        It isn&rsquo;t slow, either. The fastest delivery I&rsquo;ve watched
        recently was a cross-cutting migration everyone had put off for
        months. Two developers built it, a reviewer who knew where the risky
        code lived checked it, and the business side held the deadline. It
        shipped in <b>nine working days instead of a month</b>.
      </p>
      <p>
        My reviewer would point you to Karl Popper here. A test is a
        hypothesis you try to break, and the hardest question in engineering
        is <em>&ldquo;what should be true for this to fail?&rdquo;</em> We
        skip it for the same reason we miss the gorilla. We&rsquo;re blind to
        our blindness.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="practice" no="07" label="The practice" title="Relentless alignment">
      <p>
        Stephen Bungay&rsquo;s <i>The Art of Action</i> explains why alignment
        slips, using the Prussian army&rsquo;s answer to battlefield
        chaos.<sup><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup> Every plan runs into three
        gaps. The <em>knowledge gap</em> sits between what you&rsquo;d like to
        know and what you actually know. The <em>alignment gap</em> sits
        between what you want people to do and what they actually do. The
        <em>effects gap</em> sits between what you expect an action to achieve
        and what it achieves. The usual response is more detail, more
        instructions and more control, and Bungay shows that it makes the gaps
        worse. What works is stating the intent, having the people doing the
        work explain back how they&rsquo;ll meet it, which he calls a
        backbrief, and adjusting as results come in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The triangle runs on the same idea, and it only works if its three
        people talk often. Alignment decays under load like everything else,
        so one kickoff meeting doesn&rsquo;t hold. Here&rsquo;s the rhythm for
        a small software team, with the gap each habit closes.
      </p>

      <Checklist
        storageKey="ib-post-rituals.v1"
        markdownTitle="Relentless alignment — the rhythm"
        items={rituals}
      />
      <p class="note">The gap labels follow Bungay&rsquo;s three gaps.<sup><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup></p>

      <p>
        Each of these takes minutes. A wrong turn caught in a demo costs a
        conversation. The same wrong turn caught by a customer costs an
        incident, plus the weeks of unticketed cleanup that come after it.
        Relentless doesn&rsquo;t mean intense. It means <em>frequent</em>, the
        way a helmsman makes small corrections all the time instead of one big
        one at the end.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="close" no="08" label="The point" title="You can’t out-focus blindness">
      <p>
        Focus doesn&rsquo;t cure inattentional blindness. Focus causes it.
        Effort and care draw on the same budget, and seniority only spends it
        more efficiently on the main task. The fix that survives a real sprint
        is structural. <b>Put more than one attention system on the work,
        point each at a different question, and re-sync them often.</b>
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that&rsquo;s one developer, a technical peer who asks
        whether the code does what was meant, and a standard holder who asks
        whether what was meant is still what&rsquo;s needed. Measured against
        the weeks of rework it prevents, it&rsquo;s the fastest setup
        I&rsquo;ve seen in ten years of startups.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gorilla is already in your codebase. It&rsquo;s in mine. The
        question is whether anyone on your team is positioned to see it, or
        whether your customers are doing that job for you.
      </p>
      <p class="cta">
        If this matches your experience, or you have a counterexample, I want
        to hear it.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
